# GC98 Antibiotic Prophylaxis <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

- **Indications of ABx prophylaxis:**
  - Surgical ABx prophylaxis
  - Recurrent rheumatic fever
  - Meningococcal disease
  - Bite wound infections
  - Many more
- **Surgical ABx prophylaxis:**
  - **Rationale** - intended purpose is to prevent or reduce surgical site infections, in actuality requires weighing benefits against the risks of A/E, emergence of resistant bacteria and superinfections
  - **Indications:**
    - Procedure is associated with high risk of infection (clean-contaminated or contaminated)
    - Procedure involving implantation of prosthetic material
    - Procedures where infections are especially serious
  - **National healthcare safety network criteria for classifying wounds:**
  - **Principles of selection of ABx** - 1st or 2nd generation cephalosporin are commonly used:
    - The selected agent should have activity against <u>organisms commonly found at site of surgery</u>
    - However, the agent <u>should not be 1st-line drugs</u>, since it may compromise effectiveness in treatment
    - The agent should have a <u>narrow-spectrum</u>
    - Broad spectrum drugs like 3rd GC, fluoroquinolones and carbapenems are avoided unless there are no other options
  - **Commonly used ABx regimens:**
    - 1st generation cephalosporin - IV 1-2g cefazolin (appropriate for most surgeries)
    - If colorectal surgery/ appendectomy, consider cefoxitin (2G) for additional anaerobe coverage (alternatively cefuroxime + metronidazole)
  - **Rationale for cefazolin** - principally most studied and proven efficacy for surgical ABx prophylaxis for \> 15y:
    - Good safety profile
    - High blood and tissue levels
    - Half life (2h) longer than other 1G cephalosporins
    - Adequate conver for most common bacteria causing surgical site infections
  - **Alternative ABx regimen** - vancomycin may be used (but risk emergence of VRE):
    - Serious allergy to beta-lactams
    - Institutions with high incidence of post-operative MRSA infections
  - **Timing of surgical ABx prophylaxis** - within 30 minutes before first surgical incision (i.e. on induction of anaesthesia): 
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/risk_of_surgical_site_infection_by_timing_of_abx_prophylaxis.png)
  - **Indications for redosing:**
    - Prolonged surgery
    - Excessive blood loss (loss of ABx)
    - Redosing considered in general at 2 times the half-life of the ABx used for a patient w/ normal renal function (i.e. 4h for cefazolin)

